  "title": "Authentication vs Authorization",
  "description": "Understand the difference between authentication and authorization.",
  "order": 2,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 18,
  "tags": ["authentication", "authorization", "difference", "concepts"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Authentication and authorization are related but distinct concepts in security."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Authentication (AuthN)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Verifies WHO you are",
          "Answers: 'Are you who you claim to be?'",
          "Examples: Login, MFA, biometrics",
          "Happens first"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Authorization (AuthZ)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Determines WHAT you can do",
          "Answers: 'Are you allowed to do this?'",
          "Examples: Roles, permissions, policies",
          "Happens after authentication"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Analogy",
        "text": "Authentication is showing your ID at the door. Authorization is which rooms you can enter once inside."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "AuthN verifies identity",
          "AuthZ controls access",
          "AuthN happens first",
          "Both are essential for security"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Authentication and authorization work together."
      }
    }
  ],
